Z||000000||Monkeyman||06-07-2000||04:19 PM||||I'm about 2 steps away from selling the Grape Ape and buying an Escort.<P>I bought a set of gauges for an '87 GT from Twin Lakes Fiero (Bob Steiger).  I also installed my aux (Rally) gauges.  (Sounds good so far.  NOT)  My oil pressure gauge (Rally) bounces around and reads about 65lbs.  That's the "good" news.<P>My tach reads 1500rpms higher than the actual rpms.  Can somebody please help me?  I've just about given up.<P>I could/should just chalk it up to incompetence (haven't had a project go right yet) and go on, but I want/need to get this corrected.  Bob suggested that the oil thing might be the sending unit?  Thoughts?  No idea on the tach.<P>Help||||reg||1
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||06-07-2000||04:42 PM||||OK, don't know how much I can help, but here goes.<P>When that engine was in my car, the oil pressure was just fine, so I wouldn't think it would be the pressure sender.  Also, the tach was right as well.<P>There's a little history on the engine.  Now, checking the pressure sender and tach filter (???) would be a good idea, because who knows what the mechanic did during installation.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-07-2000||04:49 PM||||Tach worked fine until I installed these gauges.  Don't know if I screwed something up (likely) as I installed/wired the aux/Rally gauges at the same time.  Oil pressure was fine with the original gauges, too.  This is why I don't know if I did something wrong or it's just the gauges.  I got them from someone reputable and they only have 5x,xxx miles on them.  Is the tach different on a stick car as opposed to an auto?  I noticed that this gauge cluster has an up shift light (no bulb in it--maybe all '87s had it).||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||06-07-2000||06:45 PM||||Correct me if I am wrong, everyone, but I don't think the tach is different on sticks than the autos.  Maybe (I was unaware of this kind of difference) there is a difference in guages from 85 to 87??????  <P>To me it sounds like all it's going to take is a little electrical analysis since you had them working at one time and now they are not.  To pinpoint a cause of your malfunctions is, unfortunately, beyond me at this point as I haven't had that happen to me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||06-07-2000||07:08 PM||||If the tach worked before you installed the other gauges, then you must have messed something up.  Have you tried disconnecting the new gauges?  That would rule out the tach and filter.<P>Can I assume you have a good wiring diagram?<P>-L <BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-07-2000||11:21 PM||||These are new gauges (to me).  I assume that the tach worked fine before I got it, but I don't know.  I installed the "new" gauges and the aux gauges at the same time.  The tach in the last 2 gauge clusters worked fine.  How tough is it to swap tachs?  I could try the one in the last set of gauges (from an '86SE).  ||||reg||1
